{
 "id": "1972-appleyard-livable",
 "name": "Livable streets: residents' interview on traffic, neighbours and home territory",
 "description": "Donald Appleyard and Mark Lintell's street livability interview (San Francisco, 1969–70), the study behind \"Livable Streets\". Residents of three similar streets with light, moderate and heavy traffic (about 2,000, 8,000 and 16,000 vehicles a day; 12 residents per block in three age groups) were interviewed for about an hour on five criteria: traffic hazard; stress, noise and pollution; neighboring and visiting; privacy and home territory; identity and interest. Participants here answer the key question of each criterion in their own words, mark on a diagram of their street block where they live, where friends and acquaintances live, where people gather and how far their home territory extends, recall the features of their street and draw a map of it. Compare streets by friends and acquaintances per person, contacts across the street, extent of home territory, and richness of the maps. Source: Appleyard, D., & Lintell, M. (1972). The environmental quality of city streets: The residents' viewpoint. Journal of the American Institute of Planners, 38(2), 84–101; question wording follows the version printed in Highway Research Record 356 (1971), 69–84 (open copy on archive.org). No images are needed. Differences from the original study: (1) the face-to-face interview of about an hour is a self-completed page with only the published key questions, since the full interview schedule is not printed in the paper; (2) the interviewers rated each open answer on a 5-point scale, here participants also give one 5-point self-rating per criterion (wording added); (3) residents pointed at a photograph of the buildings on their own street, here they mark a generic block diagram of 10 buildings per side, which researchers can replace with a picture of their study street; (4) traffic counts, noise measurements and street observations are not part of the page; (5) tenure, age group and children questions are worded by us from the sample description. If the authors' estates or publishers object, this template will be taken down.",
 "author": "Appleyard & Lintell",
 "year": "1972",
